WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.440
A few months back, my brother Ian showed me an&nbsp;
amusing tumblr post. A link is in the description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.000
if you want to read the whole thing, but long&nbsp;
story short, this post lays out a format called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.240
Oulipo Dragon Scotsman. In ODS, participants play&nbsp;
with a 99-card card singleton deck that is led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:22.160
a Commissar, a legendary creature which can be&nbsp;
cast at any time. Okay, it’s EDH, but without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.120
any Es in the names of cards. Naturally I had to&nbsp;
do something with this information, which brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.120
us to today. Last week I instructed the people of&nbsp;
my discord server to submit a deck with no Es, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.720
to also include an approximately 100 word written&nbsp;
work to accompany it, also without Es. Was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.880
part because getting almost 400 submissions on my&nbsp;
last deckbuilding challenge just about killed me?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.640
A snail never tells. However, among the much more&nbsp;
manageable 180 submissions that these somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.680
irritating requirements produced were some really&nbsp;
delightful submissions, and in this video I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.640
to tell you about some of them and how they&nbsp;
managed the daunting task of excluding the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.200
common character in the English Alphabet. I’ll&nbsp;
also be including a number of honorable mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.400
on my website, since there were really great&nbsp;
submissions, both in decks and writing quality.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.520
A major struggle with this challenge was finding&nbsp;
decks and archetypes that aren’t too hindered by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:20.480
lack of Es. Not being able to build decks with&nbsp;
Es eliminates roughly 83% of all magic cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:26.880
so if you have 6 cards that do a particular thing,&nbsp;
on average all but one of them will be eliminated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.520
However, some types of cards had shockingly&nbsp;
few of their options eliminated. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.280
Polymorph cards—only around half of&nbsp;
all polymorph cards were eliminated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.920
leaving a respectable five left for any red blue&nbsp;
decks to enjoy, which was taken advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.800
by a neat Yasova deck that was submitted.&nbsp;
Another survivor was the card Rat Colony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.520
which allowed people to easily fill out a large&nbsp;
portion of their deck, and this was utilized by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.520
a particular Jarad deck. A third deck that was&nbsp;
relatively unhindered was Ratadrabik Nazgul combo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.000
a deck style submitted by several people. The&nbsp;
one I’m going to be highlighting here is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.960
delightfully 2000s deck—Nell’s Arcum Dagsson&nbsp;
station deck. Let’s start with the poem:

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.240
[Reading text on screen]

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:42.400
Arcum Dagsson is a spicy commander from Coldsnap,&nbsp;
and he’s one that has the potential to be quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.760
powerful in modern day unrestricted EDH. Four&nbsp;
mana for a creature that can sacrifice an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.520
artifact creature to find a nonartifact creature&nbsp;
every turn is quite potent, and the noncreature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.320
artifacts we have available to search for in&nbsp;
the year of our lord 2025 are quite horrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.400
compared to what existed back when Arcum Dagsson&nbsp;
was printed in 2006. However, what do the top tier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.720
tutoring targets like Darksteel Forge, Portal&nbsp;
to Phyrexia, and God-Pharaoh’s Statue all have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:15.040
in common? Es of course. So naturally any Arcum&nbsp;
Dagsson enjoyers had to get a bit more creative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.320
which brings me to the tutoring targets of&nbsp;
choice for this deck—Stations. There were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.680
four cards printed in Fifth Dawn with “station”&nbsp;
in the name, and it just so happens that none of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.280
them have Es. Two of them you may know as&nbsp;
classic combo pieces—Blasting Station and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.760
Grinding Station—whereas Summoning Station and&nbsp;
Salvaging Station are somewhat less popular cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.800
that only hipster youtubers play. However, when&nbsp;
the four of these are in play at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:46.080
all of their respective abilities and untap&nbsp;
clauses merge together to form an infinite combo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.960
with each other, so long as a low-cost artifact&nbsp;
for Salvaging Station is in the graveyard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.680
The two high cost stations are activated to create&nbsp;
permanents which untaps the low-cost stations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.280
and then those two are activated to sacrifice the&nbsp;
permanents and inflict harm on opponents which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.480
untaps the high cost stations, rinse&nbsp;
and repeat until opponents are dead.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.200
So the heart of this deck is these four artifacts&nbsp;
and a commander who can find them, but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.560
supports that? Well first, artifact creatures.&nbsp;
This is the resource that Arcum needs to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.320
his stations, so naturally there are 19 of them.&nbsp;
These cards span a variety of purposes, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.560
most common being helping the deck ramp. Some of&nbsp;
them are a bit filler, but with a commander like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.640
Arcum Dagsson that’s not really a problem. The&nbsp;
other key thing this deck has is protection—Arcum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.840
is a scary commander, so to keep him alive this&nbsp;
deck is packing nine counterspells and five other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.880
pieces of protection. Filling out the rest of this&nbsp;
deck is a handful of removal spells, as well as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.760
variety of other artifact-related cards. There are&nbsp;
a handful of cards that seem here specifically to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.400
synergize with certain stations—a number of&nbsp;
1 cost noncreature artifacts allow Salvaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.280
Station to come online, and there are likewise&nbsp;
a couple cards that can let artifacts untap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.160
Trading Post also lightly synergizes with a couple&nbsp;
different stations, yet another possible machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.360
that can be cobbled together. All of this has&nbsp;
a very retro energy about it, and I enjoy that.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:10.800
Moving along, something else you may note&nbsp;
about this no Es format is that it excludes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.800
a basic land—forests. This challenge did have a&nbsp;
somewhat-below-average number of green-containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.680
decks in general, but there were a surprising&nbsp;
number of people who opted to build a mono green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.960
deck. There are something like 11 lands without&nbsp;
Es that both produce green and are legal in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.920
mono green commander deck, and pairing those with&nbsp;
a modest number of green-producing mana rocks can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:38.080
get you up to a symbol count that’s an aggressive&nbsp;
mulligan policy away from being adequate. Adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.480
on an extra color makes the situation a surprising&nbsp;
amount better—it seems like nonbasic lands tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.960
have fewer Es than other card types, so there are&nbsp;
a surprising number of options within two color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:53.680
decks. That brings me to a green deck I want to&nbsp;
highlight—Shuckle’s Momir Vig biovisionary deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.480
There was actually another Momir Vig biovisionary&nbsp;
deck, shout out to the person who made that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.280
but this is the one I’m opting to&nbsp;
feature. Let’s start with the story.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:56.080
[Reading text on screen]

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.760
That story laid out a lot of what the deck is&nbsp;
doing, but let’s do a brief rundown. Momir Vig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.640
is a commander with two abilities attached—casting&nbsp;
green creature spells lets you tutor up creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.160
to the top of your library, and casting&nbsp;
blue creature spells lets you draw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.520
top card of your library. So, casting blue green&nbsp;
spells puts the cards directly into your hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.480
in classic original Ravnica fashion. Biovisionary&nbsp;
is a card that wins you the game if you have at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.280
least four copies of it in play on your endstep.&nbsp;
In a singleton format like EDH, this means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.080
some form of copying is generally required to&nbsp;
actually make it work as a wincon, and naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.560
this deck has a fair amount of that. 12 cards&nbsp;
exist to copy it in some form or other, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.920
these span a wide range of things—you’ve got the&nbsp;
basic copy plan which will take a bit of effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.720
you’ve got the stronger though non-tutorable&nbsp;
plan of using a card like Sakashima’s Will on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.320
a wide board, and you’ve got riskier plans such&nbsp;
as exiling Biovisionary under Soul Foundry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.720
hoping to be able to activate it four times.&nbsp;
As for finding each of these components,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.440
the deck features nine Simic creatures for&nbsp;
purposes of tutoring up clones, as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.560
a number of other green creatures. There are&nbsp;
also cards like Crystal Shard and Umbilicus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.520
which bounce creatures to be re-cast and can&nbsp;
also let the deck repeat ETBs on top of that.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:19.440
Outside of that, this deck features a good&nbsp;
amount of ramp, which seems like a good idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.480
for a couple reasons. For one, Momir Vig is a&nbsp;
five mana commander with a concerning effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.120
so that’s basically a seven or nine mana commander&nbsp;
if you think about it. Outside of commander tax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.760
Momir Vig also feeds the deck card advantage at a&nbsp;
healthy clip, so naturally it makes sense to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.720
sure to have a way of spending that. In terms of&nbsp;
green symbols, this deck contains 15 amongst its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:43.680
lands and two amongst its colorless ramp for&nbsp;
a grand total of 17 symbols, which certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.960
isn’t great but can be made workable enough with&nbsp;
a somewhat aggressive mulligan policy. This deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.120
certainly represents a fun navigation&nbsp;
of this challenge’s tricky requirement.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.800
A number of people went for more conceptual decks.&nbsp;
No Es is a very specific format requirement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:04.400
and asking for some sort of writing submission&nbsp;
likewise put people into a creative zone. One cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:09.040
choice was a K’rrik deck, with the deckbuilder&nbsp;
pointing out that K’rrik was canonically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:13.840
originally named Kerrick, and his choice to&nbsp;
remove the E from his name meshes nicely with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:18.960
the nature of this challenge. Another fun choice&nbsp;
was an Asmorano mardica daistina culdacar deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.080
that features an Underworld Cookbook in a&nbsp;
rule-0ed sideboard and a Wish in the mainboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:29.440
with the idea being that Asmorano mardica daistina&nbsp;
culdacar lost her cookbook and is wishing for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:35.920
return. There were also not one, not two, but&nbsp;
three X decks submitted—that’s the Unstable card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:40.240
that puts itself into your opponent’s hand.&nbsp;
The deck I’ll be highlighting is an Iron Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:47.280
deck that also chooses to engage in just a bit of&nbsp;
silver border action. Let’s start with the story.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:10:13.360
[Reading text on screen]

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.880
Iron Man himself is a 5 mana flying beater&nbsp;
who creates treasures and offers a birthing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.200
pod sort of effect for artifacts each turn.&nbsp;
Should I refer to that sort of effect as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:01.840
Oswald Effect? A repurposing effect? Anyway, turns&nbsp;
artifacts into other artifacts. The silver border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:06.960
card this deck features is Now I Know My ABCs, a&nbsp;
card that wins you the game on your upkeep if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:12.800
control permanents with all 26 letters of the&nbsp;
alphabet in the name. Iron is actually a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:18.480
choice to go with this for a couple reasons.&nbsp;
For one, treasures! That’s an E! Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:21.680
it allows the deck to go hunting for some&nbsp;
of the less common letters out there that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.640
might be attached to artifacts. Naturally, this&nbsp;
deck has a number of artifacts with less common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:33.040
characters—Tamiyo’s Journal, Blazing Torch,&nbsp;
Jhoira’s Familiar, Acquisition Octopus, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:38.400
on. There’s even Spy Kit, an equipment that gives&nbsp;
a creature the names of all legendary creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:42.160
which certainly simplifies the gameplan a bit&nbsp;
as long as you can keep the creature in play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:47.680
until your upkeep. Even outside the artifacts&nbsp;
there’s a lot of variety—we’re got Qumulox here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:53.200
for that lovely french vanilla Mirrodin&nbsp;
gameplay, as well as Grazilaxx and Xorn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.360
The core of the deck is Now I Know My ABCs, but&nbsp;
this deck has a good number of options to pivot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:01.840
into more of a conventional artifact beatdown&nbsp;
sort of plan if needed. Sharding Sphinx and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:06.400
handful of other flyers make for a solid plan&nbsp;
alongside combat damage to player effects.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.160
All of this and more is explained&nbsp;
in the deck’s 700 word primer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:15.920
also including no Es. I have to respect the&nbsp;
effort on display here—I briefly toyed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.400
the idea of writing this script E-less,&nbsp;
but quickly abandoned that idea when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.720
became clear how difficult long form writing&nbsp;
without Es is, particularly when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.680
navigating around common words I might&nbsp;
normally use like commander or deck.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:33.600
So far I’ve chosen to feature decks that have a&nbsp;
neat premise or which flaunt restrictions in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:37.840
sort of creative way. However, the final deck&nbsp;
I’m going to show off in this video is one with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:44.480
a decidedly more focused goal—Mirrorwing’s Tiamat&nbsp;
deck. I’ll let the description explain the basics.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:13:00.240
[Reading text on screen]

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:07.040
You know, it’s occurring to me that all four decks&nbsp;
I’m showcasing feature a commander that tutors for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:11.680
tools that help with some sort of unusual combo.&nbsp;
You should definitely blame 3/3 Elk for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:16.800
he helped me in selecting the top decks here.&nbsp;
Anyway, let’s talk about the commander—Tiamat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:22.480
is a five color dragon that costs seven&nbsp;
mana and tutors for five dragons. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.800
might think this means that this deck is a&nbsp;
dragon deck, and it does have two dragons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:31.200
but that’s where it decides to call it good.&nbsp;
This is because in the context of this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:37.040
Tiamat’s role is simple—he’s just the way the deck&nbsp;
turns infinite mana into a win. I say infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:42.160
mana because this is a food chain combo deck. Food&nbsp;
chain is a card that allows a player to exile a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:47.520
creature to add its mana value plus one in mana&nbsp;
that can only be spent on creatures. This combos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:52.240
with Misthollow Griffin, a four mana creature&nbsp;
that can be cast from exile, creating infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:57.200
mana that can be spent on creature spells. Once&nbsp;
infinite mana is obtained, Tiamat is then cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:02.080
searching for Dragonlord Dromoka and Hoarding&nbsp;
Broodlord. Dromoka is here for protection for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:06.640
rest of the combo, shutting off opposing spells&nbsp;
and thereby protecting the two critical creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:11.440
that will follow from creature removal. Hoarding&nbsp;
Broodlord is a dragon that gives all a player’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:18.240
spells cast from exile convoke, and on ETB tutors&nbsp;
up a card to exile that can be cast. That card is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:23.360
most likely to be Joyful Stormsculptor, a card&nbsp;
that deals 1 damage to each opponent whenever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:28.640
a convoke spell is cast. Once each of these&nbsp;
is on the field, the deck then goes back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:33.440
loop Misthollow Griffin with Food Chain a bunch&nbsp;
more times, since it now has convoke when being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:39.280
cast from exile and thus deals 1 damage to each&nbsp;
opponent per cast, eventually burning them down.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:44.240
Because Food Chain exiles as a the cost on the&nbsp;
ability and because the deck plays Dromoka before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:49.200
the other critical creatures, almost all creature&nbsp;
removal actually can’t interact with this combo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:53.760
which is pretty wild. The two things that can&nbsp;
interact with it are enchantment removal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.000
destroy Food Chain and counterspells to&nbsp;
nail key spells, and the deck naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:03.120
runs 8 counterspells to deal with that. It also&nbsp;
runs 9 cards to slow down opposing gameplans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:07.520
which makes sense to buy just a little bit of&nbsp;
time for the deck to set up. Outside of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:12.400
the rest of the deck’s cards focus on pushing&nbsp;
it to the point at which it can combo off—we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:18.800
got effectively 35 lands, 14 sources of mana&nbsp;
acceleration, 11 tutors, and 13 draw spells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:24.800
to let the deck find and play Misthollow Griffin&nbsp;
and Food Chain. A secondary benefit of Tiamat is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:30.400
that the deck has ample options for what to use&nbsp;
for these—while losing Es restricts the cardpool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:35.840
a lot, having five colors allows basically&nbsp;
all of these to still be respectable choices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:40.960
It also allows the deck to have less trouble with&nbsp;
manabase than it might otherwise—a full 8 original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:46.480
dual lands don’t contain E, and though there are&nbsp;
only two untapped fetches available, another four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:51.520
tapped fetches make this a bit less problematic.&nbsp;
Overall, this is a very fun deck to showcase the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:53.440
limits of the no-E format, though I’d probably&nbsp;
add another dragon or two as a backup plan.

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.360
So those were the decks I wanted to highlight, but&nbsp;
I’ll briefly mention a handful more that will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:02.880
in the honorable mentions. Pillowlake submitted&nbsp;
a sort of build-your-own-slicer deck—it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:07.680
a Godo deck that wants to find Assault Suit,&nbsp;
and they included a very flavorful story to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:13.440
go with it. AllusivePanda submitted a Gonti,&nbsp;
Canny Acquisitor theft deck that includes no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:17.840
green symbols whatsoever, and which needs to use&nbsp;
one of its other theft cards to steal a source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:24.080
green mana from an opponent as a result. Finally,&nbsp;
Salmon submitted a high power Glarb Doomsday deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:28.800
that’s pretty cool. There were actually several&nbsp;
doomsday decks submitted. You can check out all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:32.640
those decks at the link to my website down in&nbsp;
